    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>No contemporary source gives reliable troop counts. Jan Długosz gave the number of banners: 51 Teutonic, 50 Polish and 40 Lithuanian, but it is unclear how many men were under each.</li><li>The Lithuanian light cavalry retreated after more than an hour of fighting. Długosz called it a complete annihilation of the Lithuanian army, while some historians propose a planned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a>, and Stephen Turnbull argues that it did not quite fit that pattern.</li><li>The Order held out at Marienburg and lost little land at the Peace of Thorn, yet the indemnity it had to pay brought borrowing, higher taxes and unrest in its cities, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> were unable to turn the military victory into territorial gains.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The site of the battle was long placed in the wrong spot. Excavations between 2014 and 2019 found the main battlefield about 2 kilometres south-west of the memorial, and the mass graves found by the old chapel probably lie near the Teutonic camp.</p></aside>
    </section>

