    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1385</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> agrees in the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kreva">Kreva</a> to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, becomes Władysław II Jagiełło and creates a personal union of Poland and Lithuania</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1409</span><span class="rf-text">An uprising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> leads the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> to declare war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, and it invades Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1409</span><span class="rf-text">A truce is signed in October, due to expire on 24 June 1410, and both sides prepare for war</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1410</span><span class="rf-text">The Polish and Lithuanian armies unite at Czerwińsk, cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and enter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in early July</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1410</span><span class="rf-text">The two armies fight between Grünfelde, Tannenberg and Ludwigsdorf, and Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a> is killed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1410</span><span class="rf-text">The main Polish and Lithuanian forces reach Marienburg in late July, and the siege is lifted on 19 September</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1410</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> defeats the Order again in the Battle of Koronowo in October</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1411</span><span class="rf-text">The Peace of Thorn returns Dobrin Land to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, and the Order resigns its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> for the lifetimes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a></span></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-grunwald" data-v="543ae4d313" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The two armies form opposing lines</b> The Polish-Lithuanian army stood with Polish heavy cavalry on its left, Lithuanian light cavalry on its right and mercenaries in the centre. The Order set its heavy cavalry against the Lithuanians and waited to be attacked.</li><li><b>Vytautas attacks the Order's left flank</b> The Order had hoped the other side would attack first. Vytautas, supported by the Polish banners, went in against the Order's left flank, and the Lithuanian cavalry fought more than an hour.</li><li><b>The Lithuanian light cavalry retreats</b> After more than an hour of heavy fighting the Lithuanian light cavalry began a full retreat from the left flank of the Teutonic forces.</li><li><b>Heavy fighting breaks out between Poles and the Order</b> While the Lithuanians were retreating, Kuno von Lichtenstein's force fell on the Polish side. Six of Wallenrode's banners did not pursue the Lithuanians but joined the attack, and the royal banner of Kraków was lost and soon recaptured.</li><li><b>The king deploys his second line</b> Władysław II Jagiełło deployed his reserves, the second line of his army, against the Teutonic force that was pressing the Polish side.</li><li><b>The Grand Master's 16 banners and the last reserves</b> Von Jungingen led 16 banners, almost a third of the original Teutonic strength, to the Polish side, and the king committed his third line. The fighting reached the Polish command.</li><li><b>The Lithuanians return and strike from behind</b> The reorganised Lithuanians came back and attacked von Jungingen from the rear. The Order was by then outnumbered by the mass of Polish knights and the advancing Lithuanian cavalry.</li><li><b>Von Jungingen is killed</b> Von Jungingen tried to break through the Lithuanian lines and was killed. By one account a lance through the neck killed him.</li><li><b>The leaderless Order retreats</b> Surrounded and leaderless, the Order began to retreat. Part of the routed units made for their camp. The Order had by then been outnumbered by the Polish knights and the Lithuanian cavalry.</li><li><b>The camp is broken</b> The knights tried to make a wagon fort of the camp, but the camp followers turned against them and joined the pursuit. The defence was soon broken and the camp ravaged; by one account more knights died there than on the field.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o.jpg" alt="Władysław II Jagiełło" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jogaila_(W%C5%82adys%C5%82aw_II).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In 1230 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, a crusading military order, moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a> and launched the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prussian-crusade">Prussian Crusade</a> against the pagan Prussian clans. It conquered and converted the Prussians by the 1280s, and then turned to the pagan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. For about 100 years it raided Lithuanian lands, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>, which separated the Order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from its branch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>, but it gained very little territory.</p><p>In 1385 Grand Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> agreed in the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kreva">Kreva</a> to marry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jadwiga-of-poland">Queen Jadwiga</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and was crowned King of Poland as Władysław II Jagiełło, creating a personal union between the two realms. Lithuania's conversion removed the religious rationale for the Order's activities, and the Order's grand master publicly contested the sincerity of the conversion. Samogitia had been in Teutonic hands since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-raciaz">Peace of Raciąż</a> in 1404, and Poland had claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dobrzyn-land">Dobrzyń Land</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>. Trade also mattered: the Order controlled the lower reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-dvina">Daugava</a>.</p><p>In May 1409 an uprising began in Teutonic-held Samogitia. Lithuania supported it, and Poland announced its support for the Lithuanian cause. Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a> declared war on 6 August 1409 and invaded Greater Poland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuyavia">Kuyavia</a>, catching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> by surprise. A truce signed on 8 October 1409, mediated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-iv-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus, King of the Romans</a>, was set to expire on 24 June 1410, and both sides used the time to prepare.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers Lithuanian Crusade and Polish–Lithuanian Union, War, truce and preparations, Battle preparations, Further campaign and peace:</p><ul><li>Stephen Turnbull, Tannenberg 1410: Disaster for the Teutonic Knights (Osprey, 2003)</li><li>Mečislovas Jučas, The Battle of Grünwald (National Museum Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, 2009)</li><li>Zigmantas Kiaupa, The History of Lithuania Before 1795 (Lithuanian Institute of History, 2000)</li><li>Daniel Stone, The Polish-Lithuanian state, 1386–1795 (University of Washington Press, 2001)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great.jpg" alt="Vytautas the Great" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas the Great</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Vytautas_the_Great.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The precise number of soldiers has proven difficult to establish, and none of the contemporary sources gave reliable troop counts. Jan Długosz gave the number of banners, the principal cavalry unit: 51 for the Teutons, 50 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> and 40 for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>. It is unclear how many men were under each banner, and the structure and number of infantry and artillery units is unknown. German historians tend to present lower numbers and Polish historians higher ones. The high-end estimates of Stefan Kuczyński, 39,000 Polish and Lithuanian and 27,000 Teutonic men, are cited in Western literature as commonly accepted.</p><p>The Teutonic army was outnumbered but had advantages in discipline, military training and equipment. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> was noted, although few of its men were heavily armoured knights, and it had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombard">bombards</a>. Soldiers from twenty-two states and regions, mostly Germanic, joined it. Both armies included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a>, primarily from Silesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and Bohemian mercenaries fought on both sides.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> gathered troops from Lithuanian and Ruthenian lands, including three banners from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> under Jagiełło's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lengvenis">Lengvenis</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> contingent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and Armenian cavalry from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>. Jagiełło was overall commander but did not directly participate in the battle. Vytautas, second in command, led the Lithuanians and took part. Długosz said Zyndram of Maszkowice commanded the Polish army, which is highly doubtful; the marshal of the Crown, Zbigniew of Brzezia, more likely did.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers Opposing forces:</p><ul><li>Stephen Turnbull, Tannenberg 1410: Disaster for the Teutonic Knights (Osprey, 2003)</li><li>Mečislovas Jučas, The Battle of Grünwald (National Museum Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, 2009)</li><li>Robert Frost, The Oxford History of Poland-Lithuania: The Making of the Polish-Lithuanian Union 1385–1569 (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki.jpg" alt="Zbigniew Oleśnicki" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Oleśnicki</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Olesnicki_Zbigniew.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jan Bąkowski (1872-1934), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Polish and Lithuanian armies gathered at Czerwińsk and crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> on a pontoon bridge between 24 and 30 June, marched north on 3 July and crossed the Prussian border on 9 July. Jagiełło chose on 11 July to bypass the fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drweca">Drewenz</a> crossing by turning east. Early on 15 July the armies met on about 4 square kilometres, on a north-east to south-west axis. The Teutonic army was ready first and hoped to provoke an attack. Its armoured troops stood in the sun for hours, and light rain dampened its powder, so that only two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> shots were fired.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a>, supported by Polish banners, attacked the Teutonic left flank. After more than an hour of heavy fighting the Lithuanian light cavalry began a full retreat. Heavy fighting then broke out between Polish and Teutonic forces. The Teutonic forces concentrated on the Polish right flank, and the royal banner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> was lost and soon recaptured. Jagiełło committed his second and third lines, and von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Jungingen</a> led 16 banners, almost a third of the original Teutonic strength, to the Polish right. One knight charged the king, and his secretary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zbigniew-olesnicki">Zbigniew Oleśnicki</a> saved him.</p><p>The reorganised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> then returned and attacked von Jungingen from the rear. As he tried to break through the Lithuanian lines he was killed: Cronica conflictus says Dobiesław of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oleśnica</a> thrust a lance through his neck, while Długosz names Mszczuj of Skrzynno. Surrounded and leaderless, the Order retreated. Part of the routed units fell back to their camp, where the camp followers turned against them. The battle lasted about ten hours.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers March into Prussia, Battle preparations, Battle begins: Lithuanian attack and retreat maneuver, Battle continues: Polish–Teutonic fight:</p><ul><li>Stephen Turnbull, Tannenberg 1410: Disaster for the Teutonic Knights (Osprey, 2003)</li><li>Mečislovas Jučas, The Battle of Grünwald (National Museum Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>A note from envoys of King Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> put total casualties at 8,000 dead on both sides, but the wording is vague and may mean 16,000. A papal bull of 1412 mentioned 18,000 dead Christians. Długosz listed only 12 Polish knights killed. According to Teutonic payroll records only 1,427 men reported back to Marienburg to claim their pay, and of 1,200 men sent from Danzig only 300 returned. Between 203 and 211 of the 270 brothers of the Order who took part were killed, among them the Grand Master, the Grand Marshal and ten komturs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> had Marquard von Salzbach and Heinrich Schaumburg executed after the battle.</p><p>Several thousand were taken captive, including Dukes Konrad VII of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olesnica">Oels</a> and Casimir V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>. Most commoners and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> were released on condition that they report to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> on 11 November 1410, and only those expected to pay ransom were kept.</p><p>The Polish and Lithuanian forces stayed on the battlefield for three days and reached Marienburg only on 26 July, which gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-von-plauen">Heinrich von Plauen</a> time to organise its defence. Many Teutonic fortresses surrendered, among them Danzig, Thorn and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a>, but the besiegers suffered from lack of ammunition, low morale and dysentery, and the siege was lifted on 19 September. The Order recaptured most of the castles. Under the Peace of Thorn of February 1411 it ceded Dobrin Land and resigned its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> for the lifetimes of Jagiełło and Vytautas. It had to pay an indemnity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> in four annual instalments, borrowed heavily and raised taxes; Danzig and Thorn revolted.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers Battle begins: Lithuanian attack and retreat maneuver, Casualties and captives:</p><ul><li>Mečislovas Jučas, The Battle of Grünwald (National Museum Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, 2009)</li><li>Stephen Turnbull, Tannenberg 1410: Disaster for the Teutonic Knights (Osprey, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>There are few contemporary, reliable sources, and most were produced by the Polish. The most important is Cronica conflictus, written within a year of the battle by an eyewitness of uncertain identity; only a short 16th-century summary survives. Jan Długosz wrote his comprehensive account several decades later, and its reliability suffers from the gap and from his alleged bias against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a>. Banderia Prutenorum is a mid-15th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> with images of captured Teutonic flags. German sources include Johann von Posilge's chronicle. An anonymous letter discovered in 1963 gave details on Lithuanian manoeuvres.</p><p>Long thought to lie between the three villages, the site was placed south of Grunwald by Sven Ekdahl and by excavations of 2014 to 2017. At the old site, mass graves were found by the chapel ruins, with remains of more than 130 people in three pits and about 90 in the sacristy; Ekdahl believed the area was near the Teutonic camp. Only 28 artefacts connected with the battle were found in 1958 to 1990, and about 150 of 1,500 found by 2020 are linked to it. The surveys of 2014 to 2019 have been criticised for inconsistent publication.</p><p>The retreat of the Lithuanians is disputed. Długosz portrayed a Polish victory alone. Historians since Lastowski in 1909 have proposed a planned manoeuvre borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the German letter warned the new Grand Master against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreats</a>. Stephen Turnbull holds that it did not fit the formula, since a feigned retreat was usually staged by one or two units and followed swiftly by a counterattack.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers Names, Sources:</p><ul><li>Mečislovas Jučas, The Battle of Grünwald (National Museum Palace of the Grand Dukes of Lithuania, 2009)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle shifted the balance of power in Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and marked the rise of the Polish–Lithuanian union as the dominant regional force, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> never recovered its former power. Unrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> led to the Prussian Confederation in 1441 and the Thirteen Years' War of 1454. Grunwald is regarded as one of the most important battles in Polish and Lithuanian history. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> it is synonymous with the Grand Duchy's peak, and in the age of Romantic nationalism it inspired resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanisation">Germanization</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>. A monument by Antoni Wiwulski was unveiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> in 1910 before some 150,000 people. It is also commemorated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>, and a re-enactment is held each 15 July.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> the knights were generally seen as noble men who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and civilisation to the east. After a German victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in August 1914, fought much closer to Allenstein, Germany named it the Battle of Tannenberg and framed it as revenge for 1410. Nazi Germany portrayed its Lebensraum policies as a continuation of the Order's mission. Soviet historiography styled the battle an ethnic struggle between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and Germans.</p><p>Under Communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the state monopolised the battle's memory, and 15 July was an official memorial day until the end of the 1980s. In William Urban's summary, almost all accounts made before the 1960s were more influenced by romantic legend and nationalist propaganda than by fact. Historians have since moved towards dispassionate scholarship.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Grunwald&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy, Poland and Lithuania, Germany and Russia:</p><ul><li>Lonnie Johnson, Central Europe: Enemies, Neighbors, Friends (Oxford University Press, 1996)</li><li>Norman Davies, God's Playground. A History of Poland. The Origins to 1795 (Oxford University Press, 2005)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)<span>c. 1250</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At Grunwald on 15 July 1410 the Polish and Lithuanian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and killed Grand Master <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulrich-von-jungingen">Ulrich von Jungingen</a>. The Order withstood the siege of Marienburg and lost little land in 1411, but it never regained its former power.</em></p>

